<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">16339320</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2006</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>24</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2023</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>17</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Print">1079-5006</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Print"><Volume>60</Volume><Issue>11</Issue><PubDate><Year>2005</Year><Month>Nov</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>The journals of gerontology. Series A, Biological sciences and medical sciences</Title><ISOAbbreviation>J Gerontol A Biol Sci Med Sci</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>A new field record for bat longevity.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>1366</StartPage><EndPage>1368</EndPage><MedlinePgn>1366-8</MedlinePgn></Pagination><Abstract><AbstractText>A new longevity record has been established for a free-living bat-41 years for a male Brandt's bat, Myotis brandtii. The exceptional longevity of bats generally, and this species in particular, should make bats of special interest for researchers studying mechanisms of slow aging.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Podlutsky</LastName><ForeName>Andrej J</ForeName><Initials>AJ</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>University of Texas Health Science Center, Barshop Institute for Longevity and Aging Study, STCBM Building, Suite 3.325, 15355 Lambda Drive, San Antonio, TX 78245-3207, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Khritankov</LastName><ForeName>Alexander M</ForeName><Initials>AM</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ovodov</LastName><ForeName>Nikolai D</ForeName><Initials>ND</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Austad</LastName><ForeName>Steven N</ForeName><Initials>SN</Initials></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><GrantList CompleteYN="Y"><Grant><GrantID>AG022873</GrantID><Acronym>AG</Acronym><Agency>NIA NIH HHS</Ag